`openMemberDetails` is stable (useCallback with no deps). So that's not the issue.

Let me think about what causes individual items to re-render. The memo comparator on SingleMessageType:

```js
export const SingleMessageType = memo(SingleMessageTypeInner, (prev, next) => {
  const prevKey = getItemUniqueKey(prev.event);
  const nextKey = getItemUniqueKey(next.event);
  if (prevKey !== nextKey) return false;
  if (prev.isPreviousSameSender !== next.isPreviousSameSender) return false;
  if (prev.event === next.event) return true;
  return false;
});
```

Finishing the comparator logic...

For re-rendering to happen, either the key changes (meaning a different item shifted to that position), `isPreviousSameSender` changes, or the event object reference changes. With Insert and Set diffs in the second batch, items get shifted to new FlashList positions, but since FlashList uses `keyExtractor`, it should track by key rather than position—so the same item should stay mounted even if its position changes.

But I'm realizing FlashList actually works with positions, not keys. When items are inserted, the visible positions now contain different items entirely, so FlashList calls `renderItem` for those positions with new data. The memoization on `SingleMessageType` checks if the key and reference match, but after Insert operations, the items at visible positions have different keys, so FlashList correctly re-renders them.

The real issue might be visual flicker from the entrance animation. The component has an animation that plays on mount with opacity and translateY changes over 200ms. If FlashList unmounts and remounts components when the data array changes significantly—like going from 16 to 33 items—that entrance animation would play again, causing the flicker the user is seeing. The memoization should prevent re-renders for items with the same key and reference, but FlashList may still unmount and remount components entirely when the underlying data structure changes substantially.

Actually, looking at what the user said more carefully—they're pointing out that individual items are re-rendering, not the whole list. The two `setEvents` calls trigger two separate renders: first with 16 items, then with 33 items. When FlashList gets the new data, it needs to re-layout, and items that were already rendered get affected by this layout recalculation.

This is fundamentally a batching issue—if we only called `setEvents` once with all 33 items, FlashList would render everything in a single pass without the unnecessary re-renders. But the user reverted the debounce approach I suggested, going back to calling `rebuildEventsFromTimelineItems()` directly instead.

Looking at the system note, it seems a linter may have auto-reverted my changes rather than the user disagreeing with the approach. Still, maybe they want me to focus on explaining the root cause clearly, or perhaps there's a different way to prevent already-rendered items from re-rendering when the second batch arrives—something other than debouncing the state update itself.

The core issue is straightforward: two `setEvents` calls trigger two React renders, and on that second update, FlashList recycles cells that were already rendered, causing `renderItem` to fire again for items at positions that have shifted due to the inserts.

Even though `SingleMessageType` is memoized, FlashList still unmounts and remounts cells during these significant data changes. The solution is to consolidate those two `onUpdate` calls into a single `setEvents` call using debouncing, and I need to make sure the implementation is clean this time.

